{"title":"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(A_lot_of_Thinking_Required_dEfInItELy_)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (A lot of Thinking Required dEfInItELy )</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Three clever monkeys divide a pile of bananas. The first monkey takes some bananas from the pile, keeps three-fourths of them,\nand divides the rest equally between the other two. The second monkey takes some bananas from the pile, keeps one-fourth of\nthem, and divides the rest equally between the other two. The third monkey takes the remaining bananas from the pile, keeps\none-twelfth of them, and divides the rest equally between the other two. Given that the pile starts out with a positive number of\nbananas, each monkey receives a whole number of bananas whenever the bananas are divided, and the numbers of bananas the\nfirst, second, and third monkeys have at the end of the process are in the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, what is the least possible total for the\nnumber of bananas?\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In this solution, you start out the same as solution one. Convert everything into the fractions of largest denominator terms (this is necessary) until you get \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/f/c2f481bb5f2a17ac9d4f913b0f7a43761b58b794.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27x=11z$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"79\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/0/210c0e65f2c2879343711f890d53b54149943581.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27y=13z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>While solving, and make sure to leave a list of numbers that must divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. For example, just by looking at the basic fractions you receive from writing the starting equations, 24 divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, 8 divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. In the expression above, it's also clear that 27 divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, 13 divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and 11 divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. You might be wondering why I wrote the expression in terms of z. That's because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> has the largest divisor. The LCM of all it's divisors shows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> must be divisible by 216. The total amount of bananas can be found to equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/5/235c9d91ac25da1e6026df1fa0c575c730384a60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17z/9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"18\" />. This means there are two possible solutions under 1000: 408 and 816. Trial and error can be done quickly to find the smallest possible solution to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a74c2e26e57a3cbfd6c52596c1d3e813a24e4492.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{408}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"40\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n-jackshi2006\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28A_lot_of_Thinking_Required_dEfInItELy_.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(A_lot_of_Thinking_Required_dEfInItELy_)\">Solution 2 (A lot of Thinking Required dEfInItELy )</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (A lot of Thinking Required dEfInItELy )\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be the fraction of bananas taken by the first, second, and third monkeys respectively. Then we have the system of equations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/1/3b1a868a1e50faf7ab1b6b98f3a8b00074504c66.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{3}{4}A+\\frac{3}{8}B+\\frac{11}{24}C=\\frac{1}{2}\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"37\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/3/ec3d9add305cfe9e283abe8d158d99414b248318.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{8}A+\\frac{1}{4}B+\\frac{11}{24}C=\\frac{1}{3}\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"37\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/2/b6221848c70f54340fd4d200357f89f549aafcf7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{8}A+\\frac{3}{8}B+\\frac{2}{24}C=\\frac{1}{6}.\\]\" width=\"174\" height=\"37\" /> Solve this your favorite way to get that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dc0e99323960201e42ba8d2bf9294981426109a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(A,B,C)=\\left( \\frac{11}{51}, \\frac{13}{51}, \\frac{9}{17} \\right).\\]\" width=\"211\" height=\"43\" /> We need the amount taken by the first and second monkeys to be divisible by 8 and the third by 24 (but for the third, we already have divisibility by 9). Thus our minimum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb7cf55d57990a4a7aff423b832120a5842a85a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\cdot 51 = \\boxed{408}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"110\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>~Dhillonr25\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II\" title=\"2004 AIME II\">2004 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2004 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2004\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2004 AIME II Problems/Problem 15\">15</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\"> All AIME Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017032231\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.030 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.050 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 198/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1806/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 128/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4300/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.802      1 -total\n 59.31%    7.593      1 Template:AIME_box\n 38.86%    4.975      1 Te